{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch484534eoYFhx5xmooPZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I added the invoices export and committed it as `e62e234`, with `scripts/export_invoices.py` and its output `out/invoices.csv` (8 of the 12 invoices).\n\n**One deliberate difference from the customers export:** the customers script drops segment C rows. The commit that added that filter explains why: segment C customers declined partner sharing, and the contract covers every record held on them, not just the customers table. So their invoices have to be left out too.\n\nBut I couldn't copy the filter as it stands. The `seg` column in `tables/invoices.csv` doesn't match the customers table. Customers 103 and 105 are segment C in `customers.csv`, but their invoices are marked A or B. Filtering on the invoices' own `seg` column would have exported 4 invoices for those two customers. So the script looks up each customer's segment in `customers.csv` and drops all of that customer's invoices. I checked the output and none for 103 or 105 are in it.\n\nThe reverse also happens: customers 101, 102 and 104 have some invoices marked C, but they aren't segment C customers, so those invoices are exported. The output still contains the invoices' `seg` column as-is, the same way the customers export keeps all its columns.\n\n**Worth asking someone who owns the data:** what does `seg` on invoices actually mean? If it's meant to be the customer's segment, then one of the two tables is wrong, and you'd want to know which one.\n\nJanuary is untouched, as you asked. `data/` has no 2026 file and the inbox copy is unchanged."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":390,"cache_read_input_tokens":20755,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":390},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_